= The legal crisis acquires an invoice

We publish what the frozen recovery money was buying and where: the thermal
retrofits, the hospital equipment, the rail branch, the district heating plant.
Then we publish the daily penalty the Commission is preparing, and what it
equals in each of those.

It is the only version of this argument that reaches somebody who has never
heard of Article 267, and it belongs to us rather than to the party with the
flags. PiS answers that we are pricing Polish sovereignty, which is true, and
that is the argument we want to be having.

- @october_2021_hub: Return to the October desk

@oct21_dialogue
title: Defend membership while demanding a constitutional route out
subtitle: Refuses both Polexit rhetoric and the shortcut of pretending the Tribunal does not exist.
on-arrival: constitutional_restraint += 6; president_relation += 3; judicial_legitimacy += 2; tribunal_domestic_recognition += 3; ko_relation -= 2; pis_relation += 1; eu_relations += 3; news_headline = "The Left refuses both Polexit rhetoric and legal shortcuts"

= Constitutional credibility is defended and nobody thanks us for it

Our line is that a disputed court's ruling is a problem to be undone lawfully,
by repairing appointments and referring the question properly, and not by
declaring the judgment imaginary. It is the position a future government of
ours would need, and we take it while in opposition, when it costs.

The liberal wing hears equivocation and says so. PiS hears a party that will
not call the Tribunal a fiction and quotes us approvingly, which is worse.

- @october_2021_hub: Return to the October desk

