= October 2021 — A pit, a neighbour and a meter

The **Turów** lignite mine sits where Poland, Germany and the Czech Republic
meet. It feeds a power station supplying several per cent of the country's
electricity and it employs, directly and through contractors, most of the
working population of Bogatynia. Its licence was extended without the
environmental assessment the Czechs say the law required, and Czech villages
across the border say their wells are dropping.

Prague sued. The Court of Justice ordered the mine to stop pending judgment.
Poland refused to stop it, and the Court began charging half a million euro for
every day of refusal. The government has decided to treat a neighbour's water
table as an attack on Polish sovereignty, and Czech local officials — who
started this because of wells, not geopolitics — are now the enemy in a
national argument.

Nobody in the Sejm has a good answer, because the honest one has two halves
that hurt different people: the mine's licence was handled unlawfully, and
closing it tomorrow ends a town.

- @turow_transition
- @turow_diplomacy
- @turow_sovereignty
- @turow_workers

@turow_transition
title: Demand a lawful licence and a funded transition date for Bogatynia
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Accepts that the mine closes eventually and insists somebody budgets for the town before it does.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The party cannot cost a regional transition plan this month.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; climate_state_capacity += 5; energy_security += 2; labor_credibility += 5; workers_support += 3; rural_support += 2; eu_relations += 4; czech_relation += 6; public_trust += 3; just_transition_pledge = 1; news_headline = "The Left answers Turów with a licence, a date and a transition budget"

= A closing date is a promise to somebody

Our proposal has three numbers in it: the year the licence lawfully ends, the
money that reaches Bogatynia before then, and the number of jobs the transition
has to produce locally rather than in a ministry presentation.

The miners' union does not applaud. It does read it, which is more than it has
done with any Left document since 2015, and it notices that we put the money
before the closure date rather than after it.

